;; Docs for std/stm.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "stm.bjodoc"). The texts
;; are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Software transactional memory: change many locations as one atomic step, and wait for a condition as a CML event.")
  (see atomically atomically-evt tx-retry tx-or-else)
  (example """
    (import (std stm))

    (def from (make-loc 100))
    (def to (make-loc 0))

    ;; Move 30 from one account to the other. No thread sees the money in
    ;; both accounts or in neither.
    (atomically
     (fun (tx)
       (tx-set! tx from (- (tx-ref tx from) 30))
       (tx-set! tx to (+ (tx-ref tx to) 30))))

    ;; Wait until there is money, but not longer than one second.
    (sync (choose (atomically-evt
                   (fun (tx)
                     (if (> (tx-ref tx to) 0)
                         (tx-ref tx to)
                         (tx-retry tx))))
                  (wrap (timeout 1000) (fun (_) 0))))
    """)
  (reference """
    A @code{(Loc T)} is a location that holds one value of type T. A
    transaction is a function of one argument, a @code{Tx}. It reads and
    writes locations through the @code{Tx} with @code{tx-ref},
    @code{tx-set!}, @code{tx-update!} and @code{tx-swap!}.

    @strong{Atomic.} The writes of a transaction are kept in its log until
    it commits. The commit makes all of them visible as one step, and only
    if no other transaction has written a location that this one read. If
    another one has, the transaction runs again. So no thread sees half of
    a transaction, and a transaction does not act on values that were
    changed under it.

    @strong{Run a transaction} with @code{atomically}, which returns its
    value, or with @code{atomically-evt}, which is an event. Use
    @code{try-atomically} where a wait is not possible, for example in a
    function that .NET calls.

    @strong{Wait for a condition} with @code{tx-retry}. It stops the
    transaction. The transaction runs again when a location that it read
    has changed. @code{atomically-evt} is not available while its
    transaction retries, so a @code{choose} can wait for the condition and
    for a timeout, a channel or another transaction. If another branch wins,
    the transaction wrote nothing. A fiber that waits this way can be
    cancelled, as in any @code{sync}.

    @strong{Alternatives.} @code{(tx-or-else tx a b)} runs @code{a}. If
    @code{a} retries, its writes are undone and @code{b} runs.

    @strong{Rules for a transaction.}

    @itemlist{
      @item{It can run more than once, so it must have no side effects
        other than through its @code{Tx}: no printing, no I/O, and no change
        to other state. Do these after @code{atomically} returns, or in a
        @code{wrap} of @code{atomically-evt}.}
      @item{A transaction can not @code{sync}: it is an ordinary function.}
      @item{After a wait, a transaction runs on a pool thread while the
        fiber that waits is suspended. It sees the parameters and effect
        handlers of that fiber.}
      @item{A @code{Tx} is for one run only. An operation on a @code{Tx}
        after its run raises an exception.}
      @item{Store immutable values in a location. A change inside a mutable
        object, such as a vec, is not part of the transaction.}
    }

    @strong{Exceptions.} If a transaction raises an exception, it writes
    nothing, and @code{atomically} or the @code{sync} raises the
    exception.

    @strong{Consistency.} While a transaction runs, it can read values that
    do not belong together, because another transaction committed between
    two reads. The log is validated from time to time while the transaction
    runs, and always before it commits or raises an exception. If the
    values did not belong together, the transaction runs again. So a
    transaction never commits, and never raises an exception, from values
    that did not belong together. A transaction that loops forever on
    such values stops at the next validation.

    @strong{Progress.} Transactions that only read do not slow each other
    down: a location that a transaction only read is checked at the
    commit, not written. Transactions that conflict make each other run
    again, so under contention a transaction function runs more than once
    for each commit. Many fibers that write one location cost the most.
    Under heavy contention on a few locations, an @code{(Atomic T)} from
    @code{(std atomic)}, or one fiber that owns the state, can be faster.
    A long transaction that reads many locations can run again many times
    while short transactions write some of them.

    A transaction that reads and writes is obstruction-free, not
    lock-free. Two transactions that each read a location that the other
    writes can make each other fail at the same commit. In theory, this can
    happen again on each run. This is the cost of reads that write
    nothing. In practice, the timing of threads differs from run to run,
    and the transactions get through.

    @strong{Credits.} The design and the algorithms are those of kcas, by
    Vesa Karvonen and others (@link["https://github.com/ocaml-multicore/kcas"]),
    under the ISC licence. The commit is the multi-word compare-and-swap of
    Guerraoui, Kogan, Marathe and Zablotskiy, with read-only compares as in
    kcas. Waiting as a CML event is Bjolang's.
    """))

(:doc Loc
  (summary "A location that holds one value of type %a, for transactions.")
  (tparam %a "The type of the value.")
  (see make-loc tx-ref loc-ref))

(:doc Tx
  (summary "The log of one run of a transaction. A transaction gets it as its argument.")
  (see tx-ref tx-set! atomically))

(:doc make-loc
  (summary "A new location that holds value.")
  (arg value "The first value.")
  (returns "A new location."))

(:doc loc-ref
  (summary "The value in the location now, outside a transaction.")
  (arg loc "The location.")
  (returns "The value of the last commit that wrote the location.")
  (reference """
    Two calls of @code{loc-ref} are two separate reads. To read more than
    one location as one snapshot, read them in a transaction.
    """))

(:doc tx-ref
  (summary "The value of the location in the transaction.")
  (arg tx "The log of the transaction.")
  (arg loc "The location.")
  (returns "The value that the transaction wrote to the location, or else the value it read."))

(:doc tx-set!
  (summary "Writes value to the location in the transaction.")
  (arg tx "The log of the transaction.")
  (arg loc "The location.")
  (arg value "The new value.")
  (reference """
    Other threads see the value when the transaction commits, and not
    before.
    """))

(:doc tx-update!
  (summary "Writes f of the value of the location in the transaction, and returns the new value.")
  (arg tx "The log of the transaction.")
  (arg loc "The location.")
  (arg f "Makes the new value from the old value.")
  (returns "The value that was written."))

(:doc tx-swap!
  (summary "Writes value to the location in the transaction, and returns the value from before.")
  (arg tx "The log of the transaction.")
  (arg loc "The location.")
  (arg value "The new value.")
  (returns "The value of the location in the transaction before the call."))

(:doc tx-retry
  (summary "Stops the transaction, and runs it again when a location that it read has changed.")
  (arg tx "The log of the transaction.")
  (returns "Nothing: it does not return.")
  (example """
    ;; Take the item, or wait for one.
    (: take! (-> (Loc (Option string)) string))
    (defun (take! slot)
      (atomically
       (fun (tx)
         (match (tx-ref tx slot)
           ((Some item) (tx-set! tx slot None) item)
           (None (tx-retry tx))))))
    """)
  (reference """
    The writes of the transaction are discarded. A transaction that read no
    location and retries waits forever, as @code{never} does.

    @code{tx-retry} raises an exception that the transaction must not
    handle. If a @code{try} in the transaction catches it, the transaction
    retries all the same when it returns.
    """))

(:doc tx-or-else
  (summary "Runs first. If it retries, undoes its writes and runs second.")
  (arg tx "The log of the transaction.")
  (arg first "The transaction to try first.")
  (arg second "The transaction to run if first retries.")
  (returns "The value of the branch that did not retry.")
  (example """
    ;; Take from the first queue that has an item.
    (tx-or-else tx (fun (tx) (pop! tx high)) (fun (tx) (pop! tx low)))
    """)
  (reference """
    If second retries too, the whole @code{tx-or-else} retries. The
    transaction then waits for a change to a location that either branch
    read.
    """))

(:doc atomically-evt
  (summary "The event that runs and commits the transaction f, and gives its value.")
  (arg f "The transaction.")
  (returns "An event that is available when f commits without a retry.")
  (example """
    ;; Wait for the flag, a message, or one second.
    (sync (choose (atomically-evt
                   (fun (tx) (if (tx-ref tx flag) "flag" (tx-retry tx))))
                  (wrap (chan-recv messages) (fun (_) "message"))
                  (wrap (timeout 1000) (fun (_) "timeout"))))
    """)
  (reference """
    f runs each time the event is synced. While f retries, the event is
    not available, and f runs again when a location that it read has
    changed. If another branch of a @code{choose} wins, f has written
    nothing.

    If f raises an exception, the event is available, and the @code{sync}
    raises the exception.
    """))

(:doc atomically
  (summary "Runs and commits the transaction f, and returns its value. Waits while f retries.")
  (arg f "The transaction.")
  (returns "The value of f in the run that committed.")
  (reference """
    This is @code{(sync (atomically-evt f))} in a fiber, and
    @code{(sync/blocking (atomically-evt f))} in a function that is not a
    fiber, which blocks its thread while it waits.
    """))

(:doc try-atomically
  (summary "Runs and commits the transaction f on this thread, with no wait.")
  (arg f "The transaction.")
  (returns "(Some value) when f commits, or None when f retries.")
  (reference """
    A transaction that conflicts with another one runs again at once; only
    @code{tx-retry} gives @code{None}. This never blocks the thread, so it
    is safe in a function that .NET calls.
    """))
